(a) Emergency suspension.
(1) The chief of police shall notify the city manager that such a situation exists that constitutes a significant, present threat to public safety or public order, which is likely to continue and that requires the immediate suspension of a license;
(2) A decision to immediately suspend a license shall be jointly made by the chief of police and the city manager and shall be justified when any of the criteria set forth in section 6-70 is present and at least one identifiable aggravating circumstance exists that the city manager finds requires an emergency suspension, after consideration of the following:
a. Consistency of penalties mandated by this chapter and those previously set.
b. Likelihood of deterring future wrongdoing;
c. Impact of the offense on the community;
d. History of prior suspensions and/or revocations of licensee and of warnings of violations to licensee;
e. Disorderly conduct at the establishment;
f. Presence of illegal drugs at the establishment;
g. Violence at the establishment;
h. Gambling at the establishment; and
i. Any public safety implications of failing to immediately suspend the subject license.
j. Whether the situation in question is a violation of a supervised action plan and/or suspension previously imposed.
(3) At least within 24 hours of said decision, the city manager and/or his/her designee shall issue a show cause order notifying licensee of the decision to immediately suspend said license; notifying licensee of a time, date, and place of the review hearing to be conducted before the city manager no later than 20 days from the date of the emergency suspension; and directing licensee to show cause why said license should not be subject to further suspension, revocation or a supervised action plan; both licensee and the chief of police shall receive a copy of the show cause order;
(4) The city clerk or designee shall be authorized to issue no more than three subpoenas on behalf of each party regarding witnesses for said review hearing, unless further subpoenas are approved in writing by the city manager and/or his/her designee;
(5) At said review hearing, licensee shall be allowed to present evidence to the city manager to show cause why the license should not be further suspended, revoked or subject to a supervised action plan, and the chief of police or his designee shall be allowed to present evidence to the city manager as to why the license should be further suspended, revoked or subject to a supervised action plan;
(6) Strict evidentiary rules shall not apply to said review hearing;
(7) After consideration of all evidence presented at the emergency review hearing, the city manager shall issue a decision to either:
a. Mandate a supervised action plan;
b. Further suspend the subject license for a certain time period; and/or
c. Revoke the subject license;
(8) Within 72 hours of the conclusion of the review hearing, the city manager shall provide written notice of the decision to licensee and the chief of police. Such written notification shall set forth in reasonable detail the reasons for such action and shall notify either party of the right to appeal under the provisions of this chapter. A review of the city manager's decision may be made by appeal to the city council on the record only; no further evidentiary hearings shall be held before the city council. Such appeal shall be by written petition, filed in the office of the city clerk or designee within 15 days after entry of the city manager's final decision. It shall be the duty of the city clerk or designee to forward a copy of said appeal and the record to the city council. In order to defray administrative costs, said written petition must be accompanied by a filing fee from licensee in an amount as set forth in the applicable fee schedule.
(9) Appeal to city council.
a. The filing of an appeal after an emergency suspension process shall not act as a supersedeas, unless the city manager, so states in the written decision.
b. The city council may affirm, reverse, or modify the findings of the city manager.
c. The findings shall be affirmed unless found to be:
i. Contrary to law or ordinances, or
ii. There is no evidence in the record to support the findings of the city manager, provided, however, that the city council may modify the findings as set forth herein.
d. In the event of a modification of the findings of the city manager, the city council shall consider the following factors:
i. Consistency of penalties mandated by this chapter and those previously set;
ii. Likelihood of deterring future wrongdoing;
iii. Impact of the offense on the community;
iv. History of prior suspensions and/or revocations of licensee and of warnings of violations to licensee;
v. Disorderly conduct at the establishment;
vi. Presence of illegal drugs at the establishment;
vii. Violence at the establishment;
viii. Gambling at the establishment;
ix. Any public safety implications of failing to immediately suspend the subject license; and
x. Whether the situation in question is a violation of a supervised action plan and/or suspension previously imposed.
(10) The findings of the city council shall be final unless appealed within 30 days of the date of said findings by certiorari to the Superior Court of Gwinnett County. The filing of an appeal after an emergency hearing process shall not act as a supersedeas, unless the city council so states in its decision.
(b) Other hearings. Except as set forth in this chapter, no license shall be denied, suspended or revoked without the opportunity for a hearing as hereinafter provided.
(1) In the event that an alcohol license application is denied by the city department tasked with reviewing said application. the applicant may appeal said denial to the finance director or designee. If the applicant seeks to appeal the decision of the finance director or designee, said appeal will be heard by the city manager. The decision of the city manager shall be final, unless the applicant files an appeal/petition for review in the Gwinnett Superior Court as may otherwise be allowed by law. If the finance director or designee mandates a supervised action plan. suspends, or revokes an alcohol license, the licensee may appeal to the city manager. The decision of the city manager shall be final, unless the applicant files an appeal/petition for review in the Gwinnett Superior Court as may otherwise be allowed by law.
(2) Notice. In the event a denial of an application for an alcohol license is made, said decision shall be made in writing and shall set forth the reasons for said denial. The applicant shall be provided with a copy of the written denial within five days of the decision. Said notice shall advise the applicant of the opportunity to appeal the decision to the finance director. If the finance director or designee intends to mandate a supervised action plan, suspend, or revoke a license, s/he shall provide written notice to the licensee of said intent and shall set forth the reasons of the decision.
(3) In the event of an appeal of a denial of an application or if the finance director or designee intends to impose a supervised action plan, suspend, or revoke an existing license, the finance director or designee shall send written notice to the applicant or licensee that s/he shall show cause before the finance director or designee why said application should be granted or why said license should not be subject to a supervised action plan, suspended, or revoked. Said notice shall notify applicant or licensee and the chief of police of the time, date, and place of the hearing to be conducted before the finance director or designee.
(4) The city clerk or designee shall be authorized to issue no more than three subpoenas on behalf of each party regarding witnesses for said hearing, unless further subpoenas are approved in writing by the city manager. In the event an applicant or licensee wishes to request a continuance of any hearing. the request must be in writing and must be delivered no later than seven days prior to the hearing. The finance director or designee is authorized to deny or grant said request for good cause shown.
(5) At said hearing, applicant or licensee shall be allowed to present evidence to the finance director or designee to show cause why the application should be granted or why the license should not be subjected to a supervised action plan, suspended, or revoked. If requested, the chief of police or designee also shall be allowed to present evidence to the finance director or designee as to why the application should have been denied or why the license should be subjected to a supervised action plan, suspended, or revoked.
(6) Strict evidentiary rules shall not apply to said hearing.
(7) After consideration of all evidence presented at the hearing, the finance director or designee shall issue a decision to grant or deny the application or:
a. Mandate a supervised action plan;
b. Suspend the subject license for a certain time period; or
c. Revoke the subject license.
(8) After said hearing, the finance director or designee shall provide written notice to applicant/licensee and the chief of police of the decision. Such written notification shall set forth in reasonable detail the reasons for such action and shall notify either party of the right to appeal under the provisions of this chapter.
(9) Appeal to city manager.
a. A review of the decision of the finance director or designee may be made by appeal to the city manager on the record only; no further evidentiary hearings shall be held before the city manager. Such appeal shall be by written petition, filed in the office of the city clerk or designee within 15 days after entry of the final decision of the finance director or designee. It shall be the duty of the city clerk or designee to forward a copy of said appeal and the record to the city manager. In order to defray administrative costs, said written petition must be accompanied by a filing fee from applicant or licensee in an amount as set forth in the applicable fee schedule.
b. Except for emergency suspensions and denials of applications, the filing of any appeal to the city manager shall act as a supersedeas until such time a final decision on the appeal is entered or for a period of 45 days, whichever period of time is shorter.
c. The city manager may affirm, reverse, or modify the findings of the finance director or designee.
d. The findings of the finance director or designee shall be affirmed unless:
i. The decision is contrary to law or ordinances, or
ii. There is no evidence in the record to support the findings of the finance director or designee, provided, however, that the city manager may modify the findings as set forth herein.
e. In the event of a modification of the findings of the finance director or designee, the city manager shall consider the following factors:
i. Consistency of penalties mandated by this chapter and those previously set;
ii. Likelihood of deterring future wrongdoing;
iii. Impact of the offense on the community;
iv. Any mitigating or aggravating circumstances;
v. Any remedial or corrective steps taken by licensee or the failure by licensee to take remedial or corrective steps; and
vi. Whether the situation involves a violation of a supervised action plan and/or suspension previously imposed.
f. After review of the record, the city manager shall provide written notice to applicant/licensee and the chief of police of the decision. Such written notification shall set forth in reasonable detail the reasons for such action and shall notify either party of the right to appeal under the provisions of this chapter.
(9) The findings of the city manager shall be final unless appealed within 30 days of the date of said findings to the Superior Court of Gwinnett County. The filing of such an appeal shall not act as a supersedeas, unless the city manager so states in the decision.
(c) The requirement of service of any written decision, communication, notice, and/or supervised action plan to a licensee shall be deemed satisfied with hand-delivery of said written document to the physical location of the licensed establishment and given to any employee, contractor, and or representative of the licensee, unless the licensee requests in writing service by some other reasonable means and delivers same to the city at the time of the initial granting of the license and/or at each renewal of the license.
(Ord. No. 2017-517, Att. A, 12-11-2017; Ord. No. 2024-633, Exh. A, 8-12-2024; Ord. No. 2025-646, 4-14-2025)

227:
(a) No licensee or employee or agent of a licensee, in connection with the sale or other disposition of alcoholic beverages for consumption on the premises, shall:
(1) Offer or deliver any free alcoholic beverage to any person or group of person;
(2) Deliver more than one alcoholic beverage to one person at a time;
(3) Sell, offer to sell, or deliver to any person or group of persons any alcoholic beverage at a price less than the price regularly charged for such alcoholic beverage during the same calendar week, except at private functions not open to the public; however, this subsection shall not apply to the Historic Main Street District as set forth in section 6-87.2;
(4) Sell, offer to sell or deliver to any person or group of persons an unlimited number of alcoholic beverages during any set period of time for a fixed price, except at private functions not opened to the public;
(5) Sell, offer to sell, or deliver alcoholic beverages to any person or group of persons on any one day at prices less than those charged the general public on that day, except at private functions not opened to the public; however, this subsection shall not apply to the Historic Main Street District as set forth in section 6-87.2;
(6) Sell, offer to sell, or deliver alcoholic beverages, including malt beverages in any container which holds more than 32 fluid ounces (0.947 liters), except to two or more persons at any one time; however, this subsection shall not apply to the Old Town Overlay District;
(7) Increase the volume of alcohol contained in a drink without increasing proportionately the price regularly charged for such alcoholic beverage during the same calendar week;
(8) Encourage or permit on the licensed premises any game or contest which involves the drinking of alcoholic beverages or the awarding of alcoholic beverages as a prize.
(b) Each licensee shall maintain a schedule of the price charged for all alcoholic beverages to be served and consumed on the licensed premises or in any room or part thereof. The licensee shall not vary the schedule of prices from day to day or from hour to hour within a single day. The schedule of prices shall be effective for not less than one calendar week. This subsection shall not apply to the Old Town Overlay District.
(c) No licensee shall advertise or promote in any way, whether within or without the licensed premises, any of the practices prohibited under this section, unless it is otherwise covered by the applicable regulations contained in section 6-87.2 as set forth herein.
(d) No provision of this section shall be construed to prohibit licensees from offering free food or entertainment at any time, or to prohibit licensees from including an alcoholic beverage as part of a meal package, or to prohibit the sale or delivery of wine by the bottle or carafe when sold with meals or to more than one person.
(e) It is the intent of this section to prohibit activities typically associated with promotions referred to as "happy hour" or similarly designated promotions.
(f) The chief of police shall have responsibility for the enforcement of this chapter.
(g) Any person deemed guilty of violating this section may be punished by a fine not to exceed $200.00 and/or by imprisonment not to exceed 60 days in the county jail. Licensees may further be subject to revocation proceedings.
(h) All ordinances and parts of ordinances in conflict herewith are repealed to the extent of such conflict.
(Ord. No. 2017-517, Att. A, 12-11-2017; Ord. No. 2024-633, Exh. A, 8-12-2024)
